#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# circle_points

#% text
# `circle_points(n)` gives n points equally spaced on the unit circle, exactly, the first at angle -pi/2 + pi/n; `circle_points(r, n)` and `circle_points(c, r, n)` on other circles.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A point is a list `[x, y]`; the points go counterclockwise from the first one at the angle `-pi/2 + pi/n` (the polygon then has a horizontal edge at the bottom).

#% item
# `circle_points(r, n)` is a circle of radius `r` about the origin, `circle_points(c, r, n)` about the centre `c`. A radius written `[r, theta]` gives the angle `theta` of the first point instead of the default.

#% item
# The coordinates are exact: radicals for `n` up to 6 and for `n = 12`, `cos` and `sin` of rational multiples of `pi` for `n = 7` or `8`. `n` and `n(..., digits)` give numbers.

#% item
# The radius, the centre and the angle may be symbols. `n` is a positive integer; other values stay unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
circle_points(3)
#% output
#= [[(1/2)*3^(1/2), -1/2], [0, 1], [(-1/2)*3^(1/2), -1/2]]

#% text
# A radius written `[r, theta]` also gives the angle of the first point:

#% cell
circle_points([2, 0], 4)
#% output
#= [[2, 0], [0, 2], [-2, 0], [0, -2]]

#% cell
circle_points([1, 1], 2, 4)
#% output
#= [[1 + 2^(1/2), 1 - 2^(1/2)], [1 + 2^(1/2), 1 + 2^(1/2)], [1 - 2^(1/2), 1 + 2^(1/2)], [1 - 2^(1/2), 1 - 2^(1/2)]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# One and two points, a square and a hexagon:

#% cell
circle_points(2)
#% output
#= [[1, 0], [-1, 0]]

#% cell
circle_points(6)
#% output
#= [[1/2, (-1/2)*3^(1/2)], [1, 0], [1/2, (1/2)*3^(1/2)], [-1/2, (1/2)*3^(1/2)], [-1, 0], [-1/2, (-1/2)*3^(1/2)]]

#% text
# A pentagon has radicals, a heptagon `cos` and `sin` of multiples of `pi`:

#% cell
circle_points(5)
#% output
#= [[(5/8 - (1/8)*5^(1/2))^(1/2), (-1/4)*(1 + 5^(1/2))], [(5/8 + (1/8)*5^(1/2))^(1/2), (1/4)*(-1 + 5^(1/2))], [0, 1], [-(5/8 + (1/8)*5^(1/2))^(1/2), (1/4)*(-1 + 5^(1/2))], [-(5/8 - (1/8)*5^(1/2))^(1/2), (-1/4)*(1 + 5^(1/2))]]

#% cell
circle_points(7)
#% output
#= [[cos((5/14)*pi), -sin((5/14)*pi)], [cos((1/14)*pi), -sin((1/14)*pi)], [cos((3/14)*pi), sin((3/14)*pi)], [0, 1], [cos((11/14)*pi), sin((11/14)*pi)], [cos((15/14)*pi), sin((15/14)*pi)], [cos((19/14)*pi), sin((19/14)*pi)]]

#% text
# Symbolic radius, centre and angle:

#% cell
circle_points(r, 4)
#% output
#= [[(1/2)*r*2^(1/2), (-1/2)*r*2^(1/2)], [(1/2)*r*2^(1/2), (1/2)*r*2^(1/2)], [(-1/2)*r*2^(1/2), (1/2)*r*2^(1/2)], [(-1/2)*r*2^(1/2), (-1/2)*r*2^(1/2)]]

#% cell
circle_points([r, t], 3)
#% output
#= [[r*cos(t), r*sin(t)], [r*cos(t + (2/3)*pi), r*sin(t + (2/3)*pi)], [r*cos(t + (4/3)*pi), r*sin(t + (4/3)*pi)]]

#% cell
circle_points([2, pi/3], 3)
#% output
#= [[1, 3^(1/2)], [-2, 0], [1, -3^(1/2)]]

#% text
# Numbers in machine precision (`n(circle_points(7), 30)` gives 30 digits):

#% cell
n(circle_points(7))
#% output
#= [[0.4338837391175582, -0.9009688679024191], [0.9749279121818236, -0.2225209339563144], [0.7818314824680298, 0.6234898018587335], [0.0, 1.0], [-0.7818314824680297, 0.6234898018587336], [-0.9749279121818236, -0.22252093395631428], [-0.4338837391175583, -0.900968867902419]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The points lie on the circle; the squared distances from the centre are 1:

#% cell
simplify(map(p => total(p^2), circle_points(5)))
#% output
#= [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]

#% cell
n(map(p => total(p^2), circle_points(7)))
#% output
#= [1.0, 1.0, 0.9999999999999999, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0]

#% text
# The points are equally spaced: the distance of neighbours is `2 sin(pi/n)`:

#% cell
n(sqrt(total((circle_points(7)[1] - circle_points(7)[2])^2)) - 2*sin(pi/7))
#% output
#= 0.0

#% text
# The length of the list:

#% cell
length(circle_points(1, 1000))
#% output
#= 1000

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The number of points must be a positive integer:

#% cell
circle_points(0)
#% output
#= circle_points(0)

#% cell
circle_points(1, 3.5)
#% output
#= circle_points(1, 3.5)

#% text
# A very large number of points is not generated:

#% cell
circle_points(1, 10^6)
#% output
#= circle_points(1, 1000000)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `region_measure`
